# 115 · A hill climb's first job measures its starting point, and its guards hold against it

**Status:** `VERIFIED` · **Version:** [v0.1.0](../../roadmap.md#v010)

## What

A hill climb improves one main metric while its guard metrics must not fall more than a set amount. Its first job is a baseline: the judge measures the starting point on every cell before any change is judged, and everything after is held against those values.

- **The baseline job.** For a hill-climb run, the judge's first job is `baseline`, ahead of every other job: it builds the starting point and runs every cell several times, and records, for each cell, the starting point's value of the main metric and of every guard metric (the median and its spread), as the run's baseline. It runs again only when the starting point changes (setup, below), not when a change is promoted.
- **Nothing starts before it.** No round is planned and no trial starts until the run has its baseline, as a goal campaign waits for its first goal report. The run's page says what it waits for.
- **A starting point that can't be measured yet.** When the baseline fails (the starting point does not build, a cell crashes, or a check fails), the run is in setup: the failure, in words, goes to the workers, whose work is then to make the project build and run its measurement, within the editable paths only, never the kit. The judge checks a setup change with the baseline job itself: one that it can measure becomes the run's starting point (champion #1), and its values the baseline. Agents never judge, and the kit stays the judge's.
- **Guards against the baseline.** A guard's `max_total_regression` holds against the baseline's recorded values, not only against the sum of each step's estimate; a guard's `max_step_regression` still holds against the champion in the same block.
- **What the baseline keeps for the kit.** During the baseline job, the kit's check may write files to a folder only the judge can read and write (for example reference outputs to compare candidates against); later jobs' checks read it. Size-capped; each file's SHA-256 is in the baseline's record. Agents can't reach it.
- **In view.** The run's page shows the baseline: each cell's starting value of the main metric and the guards, and the noise; get_run and the run's history give it too.

## Why

On 2026-10-23, a speed hill climb was drafted with a separate judge-only "record run" first, because Thermite had no way for a hill climb to measure and keep its starting point's values (and a reference for its numerics guard) before judging changes. The owner: "So explain why we ran "pn-speed-record-1" instead of having this be the baseline start for a hillclimbing campaign? Why a second campaign when I would expect a hillclimb to support doing this measurement before startng experiments???" And: "Build it right now. This is a gap for v0.1.0 release. Stop pn-speed-record-1. Hillclimbs have their stat they hill climb and the stats that must not fall below a certain delta while increasing the main stat. First job should be to do a baseline run to get the seed amounts. Sometimes that might mean workers need to write code to get it working and run (Maybe they are starting from scratch)... pi-autoresearch does this well in how it does it. Maybe do a bit of research before you go off and start implementing."

What Thermite did before this goal: a hill climb's first job was a daily A/A calibration of the champion (main metric only), evaluations ran ahead of it, guards compared each change with the champion of its own block and summed those steps, nothing waited for the starting point, and a starting point that could not be measured left every attempt `errored` while lanes kept working. pi-autoresearch (MIT, David Cortés and Tobi Lütke) starts with a baseline run of the unmodified code too, but only by its prompt, its secondary metrics are not guarded, and its agent writes and may change the measurement; Thermite keeps the measurement the judge's.

## Done when

- [x] The baseline job: first for every hill-climb run, its values per cell and metric recorded, the run's rounds wait for it. Tests: `src/harness/judge/baseline.test.ts`, `src/harness/agents/scheduler-baseline.test.ts`, `src/harness/judge/judge.test.ts`.
- [x] Setup: a starting point that can't be measured gives the workers its reasons; a setup change the judge can measure becomes the starting point and its values the baseline. Tests: the same two files.
- [x] `max_total_regression` holds against the baseline's values. Tests: `src/harness/judge/decide.test.ts`, `src/harness/judge/baseline.test.ts`.
- [x] The kit's check keeps files in the baseline's judge-only folder during the baseline job, and later checks read them; agents can't reach it. Tests: `src/harness/judge/baseline.test.ts`; on a real rig, the isolation check's `baselines-list` and `baseline-by-path` probes.
- [x] The run's page, get_run and the history show the baseline. Tests: `src/control/baseline-ui.test.ts`.
- [x] The pages match the code: campaign modes, the contract and kit manifest references, write a kit, why agents never judge, verdicts and states, limits; the changelog; CREDITS.md for pi-autoresearch's part.
- [x] On the deployment: Campaign 3's hill climb starts with its baseline and no separate record run. (2026-10-24: its first job was the baseline, queued and leased at the run's start, 02:23 UTC; posted at 02:50 UTC, with each cell's median, MAD and count over 3 blocks for the main metric, the guard and the other metrics; the run waited with no round until then, and round 1 started at 02:51 UTC with two lanes. The isolation check on the rig's new images found the baselines folder closed to the agents.)

## Decisions

- 2026-10-23, owner: in v0.1.0, now (the words above). This goal first held a plan for record runs that keep their outputs; the owner's design replaces it.
- 2026-10-23, build: the baseline runs 3 blocks of the starting point alone, each running every measured cell `acceptance.runs_per_cell` times, as a candidate's blocks do; each value is the median of the blocks, with their median absolute deviation and count. No contract field: the existing one sets the runs, and contracts keep their hashes.
- 2026-10-23, build: no ledger migration. The record is events (`baseline`, `baseline_failed`), the job's spec, and the champion row of a setup change, whose `promoted_by` ends in `:setup` (and whose `champion_promoted` event has `setup: true`). A ledger from before gets its baseline when its run is next resumed.
- 2026-10-23, build: the judge judges the starting point as it judges a candidate. Whatever gives a candidate `invalid` or `crash_candidate` (a build, a crash, a check, an impossible value, a folder over its limit) is `baseline_failed`, and the run is in setup. A failure of the rig itself (a device never ready, a kit for another target) is not the starting point's: the job fails, the run waits, and the judge tries again 10 minutes later. In setup, a resume of the run measures the starting point once more, for when the cause was the rig's.
- 2026-10-23, build: the gate is in two places. The judge leases no job of a run without a baseline but a `baseline` job (`Ledger.leaseJob`), and queues its calibration only after it; the scheduler plans no round and starts no trial (`round <n> waits for the judge's baseline of the starting point (champion #<n>)` in Live).
- 2026-10-23, build: setup rounds are planned without the planner: each lane gets one experiment in the direction `setup`, at the top of the queue, and one trial, whose brief has the judge's reasons (3,000 characters at most). The judge's reasons are the plan, so a model adds nothing here. A round of the plan open when the run enters setup, or a setup round open when it leaves it, starts no new trial and ends when its trials have. Setup experiments left in the queue are dropped when setup ends.
- 2026-10-23, build: a change submitted in setup is queued as a `baseline` job (the service decides at submission, from the ledger). One the judge measures gets the new verdict `starting_point`: no verdict word fit a change that became the starting point without beating a champion. It becomes the champion through `Ledger.setStartingPoint`, a compare-and-swap like a promotion's that needs no promotable verdict. One it can't measure reuses `invalid` or `crash_candidate`. A setup change still queued when setup ended is screened as any change is.
- 2026-10-23, build: a guard against the baseline, at confirmation only, as the total check was: on each cell, the median of the candidate's values in the confirmation's blocks against the baseline's median, as a log ratio, averaged over the cells. The baseline's spread counts for the change: the allowance is spent only beyond `max_total_regression` plus the mean of log(1 + MAD / median), so the starting point's own noise never refuses a change. The sum-of-steps check stays beside it (the What says "not only"), counted from the starting point. The comparison is across time, so a rig whose speed drifts reads the drift as the change's; the step guard, paired in the same blocks, does not. Both are on the contract reference.
- 2026-10-23, build: the check's folder is `<state>/baselines/<run>/job-<id>`, the judge's, with mode `0700` on it and the folders above it; sealed after the baseline (files `0400`, folders `0500`), so later checks, which run as the judge's own process, read it and cannot change it. At most 64 MiB, 1,000 files and 32 folders deep, plain files and folders only, so every file can be named in the `baseline` event with its size and SHA-256.
- 2026-10-23, build: "the run's status line" is the sentence at the top of the run's Overview (`run-said`). The header line under the run's name is unchanged: the pages quote it (`on local-1 · hill climb · Running`) and the steps follow it.

